㊣ (your search and checker analogy) I agree, and I've saved it to memory. It also sets a rule: nothing found in the Lisp image goes into `PARSE-PROBES.tsv` or onto integration until the Python checker has passed it from an empty cache.

🈕 (where the analogy holds) In a proof assistant, search can be fast and unsound because the kernel re-checks every candidate it returns. Here the warm image can skip cold starts, keep stale caches and let us redefine functions mid-run. The Python probe then checks the result:
- from an empty cache;
- with the byte checks;
- with a test that fails before the fix;
- with a Lean citation.

㊟ (where it doesn't quite hold) A kernel checks the whole proof, but a probe only checks the constant it compiles. A fix found in the image can pass its own probe and still break another constant, as happened with `ParserFn.run` and `Array.extract`. The full sweep after each merge plays the kernel's role across all 110, so it belongs in the checker, not just the single probe.

🈖 (why the checker can't run in the image) A kernel has to be small and independent of the search. Here that means the checker can't share the image's state, so the cold Python run is the right checker even though the image runs the same code. The weak spot is the transpiler. If it mistranslates something, the image may disagree with Python. That costs search time, but it can't let a wrong fix through.

